description:
persona-core/src/spiffe_id.rs:95-102:

    pub fn provenance(&self) -> Option<&str> {
        let (prefix, source) = self.path.rsplit_once('/')?;
        if prefix.ends_with("via") || prefix == "via" {
            Some(source)
        } else {
            None
        }
    }

Two problems in one line:

1. `|| prefix == "via"` is dead code. "via".ends_with("via") is true, so the left operand already covers it. The clause can never change the result and reads as if it guards a case the author was unsure about — a maintainer will keep it forever because it looks load-bearing.

2. `ends_with("via")` is a substring test where a path-segment test is meant. The doc comment (spiffe_id.rs:91-94) says it extracts the source from a `via/{source}` tail, but a path like `user/trivia/alice` returns Some("alice") — 'trivia' ends with 'via'. Same for any segment ending in those three characters.

Rewrite — one line, no dead branch, and it says what it means:

    pub fn provenance(&self) -> Option<&str> {
        let (prefix, source) = self.path.rsplit_once('/')?;
        (prefix == "via" || prefix.ends_with("/via")).then_some(source)
    }

Note oidc.rs:89 is the only producer of `via/` paths today and provenance() has no caller yet (see the dead-surface issue), so this is free to fix now and expensive to find later.
